    "title": "Baseball: Yamamoto 3rd in Cy Young voting as Skenes wins for 1st time - NewsBreak",
    "text": "8 hours ago Los Angeles Dodgers right-hander Yoshinobu Yamamoto finished third in voting for the National League Cy Young Award on Wednesday as Paul Skenes of the Pittsburgh Pirates won unanimously.\nWorld Series MVP Yamamoto missed out on becoming the first Japanese Cy Young winner, bestowed on the league's best pitcher, after 30 Baseball Writers' Association of America members all cast their first-place votes for Skenes having rated their regular season performances.\nThe 27-year-old Yamamoto, who went 12-8 in his second big league season with a 2.49 ERA and 201 strikeouts, received 16 third-place votes. He was behind Skenes as well as Philadelphia Phillies lefty Cristopher Sanchez, who won all second-place votes.\nYamamoto's stellar 5-1 postseason, which featured two straight complete-game wins as well as three World Series victories against the Toronto Blue Jays, was not taken into account.\nRight-hander Skenes, the 2024 Rookie of the Year, led the majors with a 1.97 ERA and struck out 216 while going 10-10, winning the Cy Young for the first time at 23.\nIn the American League, the Detroit Tigers' Tarik Skubal repeated as the award winner, securing 26 first-place votes after posting a 13-6 record, striking out 241 and leading the league with a 2.21 ERA.\n==Kyodo",
